  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\nWe propose an Evolutionary Strategy (ES) with adaptive mutation to optimize the overlap integral for the Erd\u0151s problem. ES is chosen for its ability to balance global exploration with localized refinement, making it well-suited for non-convex problems. The algorithm maintains a population of candidate solutions, evolving them through mutation, selection, and adaptation of mutation step sizes to find the optimal solution.\n\n2. Mathematical specification\n- Decision variable: \\( h \\in [0,1]^n \\), where \\( \\sum h[i] = n/2 \\)\n- Objective: minimize \\( C_5 = \\max_k \\text{[h \u22c6 (1 - h)]}(k) \\)\n- Mutation: \\( h_{\\text{new}} = h_{\\text{parent}} + \\sigma \\cdot Z \\) where \\( Z \\sim \\mathcal{N}(0,I) \\)\n- Repair mechanism: After mutation, clip values to [0,1] and scale to meet sum constraint\n- Mutation step size \\( \\sigma \\) adapts based on progress, halving when no improvement is seen for 50 iterations\n\n3. Implementation procedure\na. Initialization:\n   - Use `initial_h_values` if available; else, create a default with a central pulse\n   - Resample to target grid size (280 points) using linear interpolation\n   - Clip to [0,1] and scale to ensure sum(h) = n/2\n\nb. Population setup:\n   - Population size (\u03bc) = 1\n   - Offspring count (\u03bb) = 10\n   - Maximum generations = 1000\n   - Adaptation interval = 50 generations\n\nc. Evolution loop:\n   For each generation until time budget exhausted:\n      1. Generate \u03bb offspring by mutating the parent with current \u03c3\n      2. Evaluate each offspring's overlap integral\n      3. Repair each offspring (clip and scale)\n      4. Replace worst offspring with best-performing one\n      5. Update best solution found\n      6. If no improvement in last 50 generations, halve \u03c3\n\nd. Mutation process:\n   1. For each offspring:\n      - Add Gaussian noise \\( Z \\sim \\mathcal{N}(0,I) \\) scaled by \\( \\sigma \\)\n      - Clip to [0,1] and scale to maintain sum constraint\n      - Compute overlap integral and fitness\n\ne. Termination:\n   - Stop after maximum generations or when time budget is exhausted\n   - Return the best solution found\n\n4. Search choices and useful variations\n- \u03bc = 1, \u03bb = 10 (balance between exploration and computation)\n- Initial \u03c3 = 0.1, halved every 50 generations without improvement\n- Mutation boundaries prevent out-of-range values\n- Repair ensures feasibility with each iteration\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness\n- Estimated runtime:\n   - 1000 generations with \u03bb=10: ~1000 * 10 = 10,000 operations\n   - Each fitness evaluation: ~0.1s \u2192 Total ~1000s, within budget\n- Mutation step size adaptation prevents premature convergence\n- Repair mechanism ensures all candidates are valid\n- Adaptation interval balances exploration vs. exploitation\n\n6. Validation and return contract\n- Final solution must satisfy:\n   - \\( 0 \\leq h[i] \\leq 1 \\) for all i\n   - \\( \\sum h[i] = n/2 \\)\n- Validate C\u2085 using exact task metric\n- Check for feasibility and finite values before returning\n- Include early termination to respect time constraints",
